 you to play the whole scene  out in your head and make sure that  you're making the choices  that allow the scene to crescendo in the  way that it's supposed to or play out in  the way it's supposed to so i had a  wonderful collaborator uh actor by the  name of shane dean who was doubling for  me oh so i would just communicate to him  what i was trying to do in that scene  and then we would we'd he would do it  first reverse and then eventually they'd  splice things together and it worked out  well did it affect your rehearsal  because we all had the conversation what  we would do right in that situation did  this have an effect on you i'm curious  the movie had had a deep impact on me to  the point where i needed to take a  couple of months off afterwards and just  make sure  that um  that i kind of let the character  character go a bit but in terms of just  the story itself  i think i walked away from it  very aware that i can't clone myself but  what i can do is is be very present with  the people friends family loved ones now  and and make sure i'm always taking  stock and doing and taking inventory of  the voids in my life  to work to fulfill them and to live as  whole and and be moving forward in my  life as much as possible because none of  us know  when our number is going to be called  you know we showed that scene where  glenn close explains that the clone is  you down to the molecule but what we  were all talking about we were talking  about this in the green room is  whether or not your clone jack was  actually a better version of you and  what that was like as a performer  realizing that this clone was actually  probably better to you and did not go  through what you were going through you  had a terminal illness in the film i  think i think my the clone has an  advantage it does  and that he's aware that  he's aware that he has the potential to  live on without that illness right and  so right so in some ways he's the  potential of of cameron manifested yeah  like in physical form because  he's getting to take advantage of things  in ways that cameron didn't take  advantage of because like we all like we  all do  we we take life for granted we get very  comfortable in our in our space and how  we've built a world around us and we may  not push ourselves  but upon getting this sort of death  sentence or you're aware that you're  you're you're consciously aware that  your days are numbered i think different  things can kick in for people and so  jack gets to take advantage of the  awareness that  i only have so much time but he doesn't  have the same health issues as cameron  can we just talk about you for a second  rehearsal number one on the call sheet  your first lead role yes you know when  you first came out everyone how do you  say his name how you say now everybody  knows your name and knows how to almost  everything correct  correctly guys got it down yes we do  have it down so what has this journey  been like for you how are you feeling  about being mahershali and all that that  means  so from there i i feel i feel so blessed  everything happens  happens and for me my interpretation is  things happen in god's time  and sometimes we we want things when we  want them and we think we think we're  ready for them but  when i look back i don't think i was  ready for this moment and  this degree of responsibility and i feel  very very grateful that that things have  begun to happen for me and the time in  which they have because um  i feel like i can be in gratitude about  it all in a different way than what i  could have been years earlier last thing  about this movie real quick it captures  the the duality in all of us yes and  there's one person that is dealing with  the mortality of who he is and another  person has a very optimistic view on the  world  what advice would you give to  individuals out there that might be  dealing with two versions of themselves  and are  grappling with the idea of being better